{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_14-15-46-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft; python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nimport json\nd={\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"The receipt qualifies my source-only part-answer in the honest direction: the FSM's latency does reach the main loop, but not as a stall proportional to how far the FSM has fallen behind. With a 25ms Apply the main-loop probe took 6ms against a 0ms baseline while all 400 applies were eventually accepted, and the backlog at the probe was one entry rather than a queue; with a fast FSM the same flood left the probe at zero. So the loop is starved per batch and the lag does not accumulate, because the client's own Apply calls are throttled by the same pipeline - which is what the earlier reading, phrased as non-progress lasting as long as the FSM lags, over-stated.\\\",\n \\\"investigations\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"ab0cc02efafd4a8ab13a9d1118e251e9-question-0\\\",\\\"disposition\\\":\\\"explained\\\"}],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[{\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"ab0cc02efafd4a8ab13a9d1118e251e9-question-0\\\",\n  \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"eb2a50040af94c36b2e9baea2244d135-experiment\\\",\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"raft_applybatch_send\\\",\\\"api_channel\\\",\\\"fsm_consumer\\\",\\\"fsm_apply_contract\\\",\\\"api_shutdown_closes_channel\\\"],\n  \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"9de4a9fc93b649229353b41e6def41e5\\\":[0,1,2,3]},\n  \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"holds\\\",\\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"refuted\\\",\n  \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"ab0cc02efafd4a8ab13a9d1118e251e9-knowledge-0\\\",\\\"revision\\\":1,\n   \\\"assertion\\\":\\\"The entry says the library transfers the FSM's latency into the main loop with no rejection path; the measurement shows that transfer is real but bounded per batch rather than by the accumulated backlog.\\\"}],\n  \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Retained execution (check 9de4a9fc93b649229353b41e6def41e5, exit 0, tests_passed, 4 parsed events, 0 parse errors; two single-voter clusters, 400 applies fired without waiting for their futures). Both declared comparisons hold and are complete. In the slow trial the FSM slept 25ms inside every Apply, the flood was fully accepted, the backlog between last log index and last applied index at the probe was one entry, and VerifyLeader - answered inside the main loop for a single voter - took 6ms against an idle baseline of 0ms. In the control the FSM applied without sleeping, the same flood ran, the backlog was zero and the probe took 0ms, so nothing but the FSM's own latency distinguishes the two trials. The question asked whether a slow FSM stops the main loop long enough to stop RPC processing and the lease check, and whether anything bounds it; the answer from this run is that the leak is real and bounded: the main loop waits about one FSM apply per batch it delivers, which for a 25ms FSM showed up as a few milliseconds of extra probe latency, and the lag does not build, because the pipeline throttles the client's own Apply calls instead of accumulating a queue - the flood took seconds to be accepted rather than being posted and forgotten. The stronger reading I had recorded as a source-only part-answer, that non-progress lasts as long as the FSM lags behind its commits, is therefore narrowed: what lasts is the per-batch wait, and the accumulated lag stays at the boundary the client feeds. The suspicion that a slow FSM stalls the leader materially is refuted for this configuration, with the residual that the throttle is the client's own call path.\\\",\n  \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\\\"The throttle that keeps the lag at one entry is the unbuffered apply channel plus a caller that waits: a deployment using BatchApplyCh buffers that channel so callers can post without waiting, and this run did not exercise that option, which is the configuration in which a queue could accumulate.\\\",\n   \\\"A multi-node cluster adds replication to the picture and was not measured here; the probe used a single voter so its path to the main loop is unambiguous.\\\",\n   \\\"The probe latency is a few milliseconds against a 25ms FSM, which is within scheduling noise for one trial per configuration; the comparison is between the two trials rather than a rate measured over repetitions.\\\",\n   \\\"No application read is served and no lease check was observed directly, so the claim about RPC processing and leases is inferred from the probe's path through the main loop.\\\"],\n  \\\"limitations\\\":[\\\"Two configurations (25ms and no delay) on single-voter in-memory clusters; the FSM delay was not swept.\\\",\n   \\\"One trial each, so the magnitudes are indicative rather than measured rates.\\\",\n   \\\"The harvested operands are the probe latency, the backlog at one instant, and the apply count; the harness does not sample the channel occupancy or the lease timer.\\\"]}]\n}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent=2)\nPYEOF\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_14-15-46-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -3; date -u +%H:%M:%S\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
